86a5ac" 13194139535799 17592186045443 true] [17592186529263 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529308 13194139535799 17592186045443 true] [17592186529263 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535799 17592186045443 true] [17592186529263 1071 "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$\\{y + 2x + 4 = 0; 4y - 4x + 9 = 0$\n\nThis system of equations has {{field-1}}." 13194139535799 17592186045443 true] [17592186529263 1072 #uuid "22c76199-015b-5355-8da5-51b3e4eb6386" 13194139535799 17592186045443 true] [17592186529263 1074 "Let's start by solving the system using elimination.\n\nFirst, rearrange each equation to put the variable terms on the left and the constant on the right:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x + y &= -4 \\\\\n-4x + 4y &= -9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that if we multiply the first equation by $2$, then we will get a $4x$ term that can cancel with the $-4x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\{2x + y = -4 \\mid \\mid \\cdot (2); -4x + 4y = -9\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, the $x$ terms cancel, and we can solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4x + 2y &= -8 \\\\\n-4x + 4y &= -9 \\\\\n6y &= (-8 - 9) \\\\\n6y &= -17 \\\\\ny &= - \\frac{17}{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the first equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x + (1)(- \\frac{17}{6}) &= -4 \\\\\n2x &= - \\frac{7}{6} \\\\\nx &= - \\frac{7}{12}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system has *a single solution*, $(- \\frac{7}{12},- \\frac{17}{6})$." 13194139535799 17592186045443 true] [17592186529264 1080 "a single solution" 13194139535799 17592186045443 true] [17592186529264 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139535799 17592186045443 true] [17592186529264 1083 #uuid "26412d41-7859-567a-8d0b-616fdfaae78b" 13194139535799 17592186045443 true] [17592186529265 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529281 13194139535799 17592186045443 true] [17592186529265 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535799 17592186045443 true] [17592186529265 1071 "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$\\{-4x + 4y = -9; 2x - y = -4$\n\nThis system of equations has {{field-1}}." 13194139535799 17592186045443 true] [17592186529265 1072 #uuid "27a6ba63-3c85-5feb-bfac-b122a5e9f9a3" 13194139535799 17592186045443 true] [17592186529265 1074 "Let's start by solving the system using elimination.\n\nNotice that if we multiply the second equation by $2$, then we will get a $4x$ term that can cancel with the $-4x$ term in the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\{-4x + 4y = -9; 2x - y = -4 \\mid \\mid \\cdot (2)\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, the $x$ terms cancel, and we can solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-4x + 4y &= -9 \\\\\n4x - 2y &= -8 \\\\\n2y &= (-9 - 8) \\\\\n2y &= -17 \\\\\ny &= - \\frac{17}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the first equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-4x + (4)(- \\frac{17}{2}) &= -9 \\\\\n-4x &= 25 \\\\\nx &= - \\frac{25}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system has *a single solution*, $(- \\frac{25}{4},- \\frac{17}{2})$." 13194139535799 17592186045443 true] [17592186529266 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529278 13194139535799 17592186045443 true] [17592186529266 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535799 17592186045443 true] [17592186529266 1071 "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$\\{y + 2x + 2 = 0; 4y + 8x + 8 = 0$\n\nThis system of equations has {{field-1}}." 13194139535799 17592186045443 true] [17592186529266 1072 #uuid "31a5b8b3-2787-5aca-9212-729c0bca425f" 13194139535799 17592186045443 true] [17592186529266 1074 "Let's start by solving the system using elimination.\n\nFirst, rearrange each equation to put the variable terms on the left and the constant on the right:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x + y &= -2 \\\\\n8x + 4y &= -8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that if we multiply the first equation by $-4$, then we will get a $-8x$ term that can cancel with the $8x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\{2x + y = -2 \\mid \\mid \\cdot (-4); 8x + 4y = -8\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, it turns out that both variables cancel, and we obtain a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-8x - 4y &= 8 \\\\\n8x + 4y &= -8 \\\\\n0 &= (8 - 8) \\\\\n0 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe statement $0=0$ is *always true*. Therefore, we conclude that the system has *infinitely many solutions*." 13194139535799 17592186045443 true] [17592186529267 1080 "no solutions" 13194139535799 17592186045443 true] [17592186529267 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139535799 17592186045443 true] [17592186529267 1083 #uuid "31e0880c-27b5-5a13-87e6-3705af860da9" 13194139535799 17592186045443 true] [17592186529268 1080 "a single solution" 13194139535799 17592186045443 true] [17592186529268 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139535799 17592186045443 true] [17592186529268 1083 #uuid "344db828-cf79-52e5-a777-8ab84b874966" 13194139535799 17592186045443 true] [17592186529269 1075 #edb/ref 17592186529275 13194139535799 17592186045443 true] [17592186529269 1075 #edb/ref 17592186529312 13194139535799 17592186045443 true] [17592186529269 1075 #edb/ref 17592186529314 13194139535799 17592186045443 true] [17592186529269 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 13194139535799 17592186045443 true] [17592186529269 1077 "field-1" 13194139535799 17592186045443 true] [17592186529269 1078 #edb/ref 17592186529312 13194139535799 17592186045443 true] [17592186529269 1079 #uuid "431a38a6-e58c-5853-ad1f-a6fc6bb01e65" 13194139535799 17592186045443 true] [17592186529270 1075 #edb/ref 17592186529264 13194139535799 17592186045443 true] [17592186529270 1075 #edb/ref 17592186529291 13194139535799 17592186045443 true] [17592186529270 1075 #edb/ref 17592186529328 13194139535799 17592186045443 true] [17592186529270 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 13194139535799 17592186045443 true] [17592186529270 1077 "field-1" 13194139535799 17592186045443 true] [17592186529270 1078 #edb/ref 17592186529291 13194139535799 17592186045443 true] [17592186529270 1079 #uuid "43939bab-211c-5b1e-9dcd-127b5f8c732a" 13194139535799 17592186045443 true] [17592186529271 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529326 13194139535799 17592186045